Walk me through how you write unit tests and integration tests for your project.
The code I just shared is a real example from our TeamSync backend. For unit tests, I targeted the core drag-and-drop reorder logic in TaskService — I mocked out TaskRepository with Mockito so what's being tested is purely the business logic: does creating a task with a blank title throw an exception, does reordering correctly update the position — none of it touches a real database, so it runs in milliseconds, which lets us run the full suite on every single PR. For integration tests, I used Spring Boot Test with Testcontainers to spin up a real, ephemeral Postgres container, exercising the full path from HTTP request through the controller, service, repository, and into the database. That layer verifies the components genuinely work together — does a bad request body actually return a 400 instead of a 500, does the task list come back correctly ordered by position — things a unit test simply can't catch, because they depend on serialization, database constraints, and Spring's autoconfiguration all being wired up correctly in a real environment.
What are TDD, BDD, and DDD, and how are they different?
These three get lumped together a lot, but they actually operate at completely different levels. TDD is Test-Driven Development, and it's about the rhythm of writing code — you write a failing test first, then write just enough code to make it pass, then refactor. It's a red-green-refactor loop. That's exactly how we implemented the reorderTask logic — I wrote the test "given an existing task, calling reorder should update its position" first, watched it fail, and only then implemented the logic. BDD is Behavior-Driven Development, and it's not about how you write code, it's about describing the system's expected behavior in business language, usually in a Given-When-Then format. The advantage is that even a PO or a QA who doesn't write code can read and understand these scenarios — it bridges business and engineering language, and honestly our acceptance criteria often end up written in that exact structure without us even calling it BDD. DDD is Domain-Driven Design, and that has nothing to do with a testing methodology — it's an architectural design approach where you model the software around the business domain itself, carving out clear bounded contexts and using a shared, consistent language across code and conversations. When we split TeamSync into Task, User, and Board as fairly independent domain modules, that's essentially us borrowing from DDD thinking.
What is JUnit?
JUnit is the dominant unit testing framework in the Java ecosystem. It gives you the standard structure for writing tests — annotating a method with @Test, using @BeforeEach to set up state before each test runs, and a full set of assertion methods like assertEquals and assertThrows to verify the result matches what you expect. All of our Java backend tests are built on JUnit 5.
What is Mockito?
Mockito is a mocking framework that pairs with JUnit. Its job is to fake out the other components that the code under test depends on, instead of actually initializing them. When testing TaskService, I don't want to actually connect to a database, so I mock out TaskRepository with Mockito — using when(...).thenReturn(...) to control what it returns when called, and verify(...) to confirm a method was actually invoked the right number of times with the right arguments. That's how the test stays fast and reliable instead of failing just because a database wasn't reachable.
How do you actually test your application in practice?
We follow a testing pyramid approach — a large base of unit tests covering business logic, fast, written with JUnit and Mockito; a smaller layer of integration tests verifying the service, database, and external dependencies actually work together, fewer of these since they're slower to run; and at the top, a thin layer of end-to-end tests covering only the most critical user paths — login, creating a task, drag-and-drop reordering — the fewest of all, since they're the most expensive to maintain. All of these are wired into our CI pipeline. Unit and integration tests are a hard gate before any PR can merge; end-to-end tests take longer to run, so we run those as a follow-up pass after merging to main.
What tools do you use for code quality analysis?
For our Java backend, we use SonarQube — it flags code smells, duplication, methods with too-high cyclomatic complexity, and it lets us set a quality gate, like blocking a PR if test coverage drops below a certain percentage. On the frontend we use ESLint and Prettier to keep code style consistent. That said, I think static analysis tools are only one layer — code review itself is what really matters. Tools catch mechanical issues, but whether the architecture makes sense or the naming is clear still needs a human looking at it.
What's the difference between Authentication and Authorization?
I usually answer this with a simple frame: Authentication verifies who you are, Authorization verifies what you're allowed to do. When a user types in their username and password to log in, that's authentication — the system confirms you really are Sarah. But whether Sarah is allowed to delete a board, or edit someone else's task, that's authorization, based on her role or permissions. In our project, login uses JWT for authentication, and actual permission checks happen at the API layer based on the user's role.
What's the difference between Encryption, Hashing, and Encoding?
These three get mixed up a lot, but they serve completely different purposes. Encoding is a reversible transformation meant for compatibility, not security — like Base64. Anyone who gets the encoded content can easily convert it back to the original, no key involved. Encryption is also reversible, but it requires a key to unlock — like AES or RSA — and its purpose is confidentiality; we encrypt sensitive data in transit. Hashing is one-way and irreversible, used for integrity checks or password storage — like bcrypt or SHA-256. We never store user passwords in plain text; we store the hash, so even if the database leaks, an attacker only gets the hash, which in theory can't be reversed back to the original password — as long as you use a salted, rainbow-table-resistant algorithm like bcrypt rather than something like plain MD5.
How do you secure your application?
I'd cover a few layers: HTTPS/TLS enforced at the transport layer; passwords stored as salted bcrypt hashes, never plain text; authentication handled statelessly with JWT, and authorization done as fine-grained, role-based checks; all user input validated and sanitized to prevent SQL injection and XSS; secrets and sensitive config never hardcoded, always injected at runtime from something like Secrets Manager; rate limiting at the API Gateway layer to prevent abuse; dependencies regularly scanned for known vulnerabilities with a tool like Trivy; and IAM roles for each cloud service scoped tightly following least-privilege.
What is JWT?
JWT stands for JSON Web Token — a self-contained token format made up of a header, payload, and signature, separated by dots. It's used for stateless authentication: once a user logs in successfully, the server generates a signed JWT and hands it back to the client, and on every subsequent request the client includes it in the Authorization header. The server just verifies the signature is valid to confirm the user's identity and whatever claims are in the token — no server-side session store needed, which is particularly friendly for a horizontally-scaled microservices setup like ours.
What is OAuth2?
OAuth2 is an authorization framework, and the core problem it solves is letting a third-party application access some of your resources without ever knowing your password. The classic example is "Login with Google," or a service needing to call another service's API without directly sharing credentials. It defines several grant flows — the Authorization Code flow fits scenarios with frontend interaction, the Client Credentials flow fits service-to-service calls. One thing worth being precise about: OAuth2 and JWT aren't the same thing — OAuth2 is a protocol defining how you obtain access, while JWT is just one specific token format frequently used to carry that access information within the process. They're often paired together, but they're not conceptually equivalent.


